#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153183} :activity/id #uuid "8a4d8167-cbc1-5987-8890-23de47beaf31" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050800 :topic/id #uuid "67f29868-b7dd-582e-8c65-4c6fd95a9741" :topic/math-academy-id 2614} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153045 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152150 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151938 :question/id #uuid "e49f7799-2724-5fe0-999f-bfa9ab5ce610" :question/math-academy-id "e-6244"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "34f7fe39-9ca2-5c27-8852-96eb58a51f62" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152398} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152712} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256779} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345024} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345038} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345040} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345041}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Upper Riemann Sum in the Case of a Strictly Increasing Function"} :step/id #uuid "cf8a0158-b0bb-51d1-a026-029d210bf549" :step/math-academy-id 23449} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153046 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152151 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151939 :question/id #uuid "1ac785c8-3d14-5968-94d3-6f4588f08d91" :question/math-academy-id "e-6245"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4cd571d2-a2d6-534d-84dd-a8182f64d1d6" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048206} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049573}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152713} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152719} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259497} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259498} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259499} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345052}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Upper Riemann Sum in the Case of a Strictly Decreasing Function"} :step/id #uuid "171bad61-0332-58ad-81bd-9b1ee0d315e8" :step/math-academy-id 23450 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153045}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153047 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152152 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151940 :question/id #uuid "14e77998-b24b-56c1-8901-dc94d3ef98a5" :question/math-academy-id "e-6246"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9fa8da8f-00bb-5663-b578-99086398a1b7" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048363} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049629} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049878}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152394} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152395} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256773} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256774} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256775} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256776}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Expression for the Upper Bound in Terms of Maximum Values"} :step/id #uuid "cfacb02d-e3c0-53df-a1ea-8dac614072e6" :step/math-academy-id 23451 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153051}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153051 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153350 :tutorial/content "Recall the region $R$ that we encountered earlier:\n\n![](images/20/2026210e2cd58b2f2f7290a2d4f4186875903d146d02597787393727afd7f6a6.png)\n\nEarlier, we saw that an upper bound for the volume $V$ of \n\n$$\nz = f(x,y) = 3-x^2-y^2\n$$\n\n \n\ndefined over the region $R$ with respect to the partition $P$ (shown above) is given by\n\n$$\nU = \\dfrac14(M_{11} + M_{21}+ M_{12} + M_{22}),\n$$\n\nwhere $M_{ij}$ is the maximum value of $f$ over the subregion $R_{ij}.$ Our goal now is to calculate the values of $M_{ij}.$\n\nWe start by considering $M_{11},$ so let's zoom in on $R_{11}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/04/042a236318a636de2fbfd9522dd221dbfd47906e63bc2290ca554bbcfccec958.png)\n\nNow, let's take a closer look at the function $f(x,y)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = 3-x^2-y^2\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that\n\n- as we *increase* $x,$ the value of $f$ *decreases* (because we're *subtracting* $x^2$), and\n\n- as we *increase* $y,$ the value of $f$ *decreases* (because we're *subtracting* $y^2$).\n\n![](images/4e/4e29408793329549172cdcc7113ec1d356d5b78e07b59b71e0d5284149375442.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in this subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of the subregion.\n\n![](images/4f/4fb0e98a83794b8e5a105e3b1afaa19bf28be1f8986c958177b2bb3b10f3bb28.png)\n\nSince the function $f(x,y)$ decreases as $x$ and $y$ increase everywhere on $R,$ the maximum value of $f$ in each subregion $R_{ij}$ *always* occurs at the bottom left corner! \n\n![](images/aa/aaeb4af429c9509b05377954d7c114fd19098477065cceb92b219c6e62703671.png)\n\nCalculating the value of $f$ at each bottom left corner, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{11} & =f(0,0)=3 \\\\ {M}_{21} & =f(\\frac{1}{2},0)=2.75 \\\\ {M}_{12} & =f(0,\\frac{1}{2})=2.75 \\\\ {M}_{22} & =f(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2})=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above values in our expression for $U,$ we get\n\n$$\nU = \\dfrac14(3+2.75+2.75+2.5) = 2.75.\n$$\n\nRemember that $U$ is the *upper* Riemann sum, which is an *upper* bound for the volume between $z=f(x,y)$ and the region $R.$ Therefore, we conclude that the volume between $z = f(x,y)$ and the region $R$ must be smaller than (or equal to) $2.75.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "2abddec2-887d-532c-8ebc-62149543755e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3957 :tutorial/title "Calculating the Maximum Value of the Function in Each Partition"} :step/id #uuid "b0239999-1cf0-5570-9e21-786f635fe397" :step/math-academy-id 23472 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153185}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153183 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153417 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we will describe a process for calculating an **upper bound** for the volume enclosed between a surface $z=f(x,y)$ and the $xy$-plane. \n\nAs an example, consider the rectangle $R$ in the $xy$-plane, defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA sketch of the region $R$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/bf/bf7ef4752b0910b5544707eef97c854d8eeeedbc221a81c5fa631cf38b8b8d9a.png)\n\nNow consider the function $f(x,y) = 3-x^2-y^2,$ defined over $R,$ shown below.\n\n![](images/34/34f7936b29e6a177ae0c831e36654dc5059514580a1947324a40d52320b83e5a.png)\n\nThe space underneath the surface and above the region $R$ defines a fixed volume $V.$ Our goal is to find an upper bound for $V.$ We do this by approximating this volume using rectangular solids. \n\nWe start by partitioning the $x$- and $y$-intervals of $R.$ Let's pick two subintervals of equal length for both $x$ and $y.$\n\n![](images/c3/c375b605ca6b7d7f2935218976f5e09db5ed2cfb64d811b1fe0a854d02646a36.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is now partitioned into $4$ rectangles:\n\n![](images/20/2026210e2cd58b2f2f7290a2d4f4186875903d146d02597787393727afd7f6a6.png)\n\nWe seek an *upper* bound for the volume $V.$ So, we let $M_{ij}$ be the *largest* value of $f$ in each subregion $R_{ij}.$ We then define a rectangular solid in each subregion whose base is $R_{ij}$ and whose height is $M_{ij}.$ \n\nFor example, the rectangular solid corresponding to $R_{11}$ is given below. \n\n![](images/62/62cee54b71235dc75cab863f0971735ec221fc61a022edf78994ea3f935c1cd4.png)\n\nThe volume of this rectangular solid is\n\n$$\nM_{11}\\cdot \\textrm{Area}(R_{11})  = \\dfrac14M_{11}.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, the volumes of the rectangular solids formed by the partitions $R_{21}, R_{12},$ and $R_{22}$ respectively are\n\n$$\n\\dfrac14M_{21}, \\qquad \\dfrac14M_{12}, \\qquad \\dfrac14M_{22}.\n$$\n\nDrawing all four rectangular solids, we get a volume that looks as follows:\n\n![](images/f8/f8f44d8a180838cb2b79b775483260ea9a30608cb8a618eaf86a5edcabd2c610.png)\n\nThe upper bound $U$ for our volume is the sum of the volumes of our four rectangular solids. This gives\n\n$$\nU = \\dfrac14(M_{11} + M_{21} + M_{12} + M_{22}).\n$$\n\nThe number $U$ is called the **upper Riemann sum with respect to the given partition**.\n\n**Note:** We don't yet know the values of $M_{ij},$ the height of each solid. We'll learn to figure these out a little later in the lesson. For now, we will just focus on writing $U$ as an expression of $M_{ij}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "aca556d7-abe9-5c2e-a21c-a1abde3b1b37" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5777 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "883dc60d-baeb-5e5a-83a1-e45e3ddfdcfc" :step/math-academy-id 35488 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153047}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153185 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153419 :tutorial/content "We need to introduce just a bit more notation. To help us, let's consider the region $R$ and the partition $P$ shown below.\n\n![](images/5d/5d5af7cb2f2b12a52bb624d12938aafd2d34b079ad5d28dc44133292b98956ec.png)\n\nIt's convenient to express our region $R$ in a way that does not involve a diagram. We can do this using the Cartesian product.\n\nUsing the Cartesian product, we have $R = [1,4]\\times [1,3].$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- The partition of the $x$-interval $[1,4]$ can be expressed as $P_1 = \\{1,2,3,4\\}.$\n\n- The partition of the $y$-interval $[1,3]$ can be expressed as $P_2 = \\{1,3\\}.$\n\n- Therefore, we can write the partition as $P = P_1\\times P_2.$\n\nFinally, we write $U(f,P)$ for the upper Riemann sum of the function $f$ with respect to $P.$\n\n**Note:** We write $U(f,P)$ because the answer we get for the upper bound $U$ depends on the function $f$ and the partition $P$ that we choose. If we choose a different function or partition, then we'll get a different upper bound." :tutorial/id #uuid "5627ca82-fd4e-522e-abb5-8d82d82137eb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5783 :tutorial/title "Updating Our Notation"} :step/id #uuid "32dfda1a-8765-544a-8839-29e132fa4334" :step/math-academy-id 35509 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153046}}] :activity/title "Approximating Volumes Using Upper Riemann Sums" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186152326}]}
